WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.561
- [Announcer] This Great Big
Story was made possible by IBM.

00:00:03.561 --> 00:00:07.023
(birds chirping)

00:00:07.023 --> 00:00:08.510
(children chattering)
(inspiring music)

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.150
- Every child is a scientist,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.850
and just like everywhere in the world,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.330
every child in Africa is very curious,

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.640
likes to experiment, likes to discover.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.940
Imagine every single student
on the African continent

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.170
having their own science lab

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.540
with the microscopes and
then the circuit boards

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.790
and carrying that science lab

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.115
with them everywhere they go.

00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:31.948
(inspiring music)

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.240
I'm Charles Ofori Antipem.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.960
I'm 26 years old; I'm a scientist,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.680
and some people call me The Mad Scientist.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.740
- [Narrator] He might be mad,
but he sure is brilliant.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.180
To tackle the lack of
science education in Ghana,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.390
Charles and his team took a science lab

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.210
and just kind of shrunk it.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.860
- Back in 2015, my
co-founder Michael and I

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.310
really wanted to find a solution

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.960
and that was when we
built the first prototype

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.660
of the Science Set.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.330
The Science Set is a
lab that is small enough

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.820
that it can fit on the desk of a student

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.530
and affordable enough that every student

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.800
can simply have one.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.570
The Science Set currently costs $20.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.060
It has close to 45 individual
components inside it;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.850
and those students can use to perform

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.049
a wide variety of activities.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:24.216
(children chattering)

00:01:26.793 --> 00:01:27.964
- [Woman] Good, clap for ourselves.

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:30.071
(clapping)

00:01:30.071 --> 00:01:32.530
Now, the second activity ...

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.210
- I can even use the Science Set

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.920
or anything in the Science Set

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.740
to make anything that can help people.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.150
- I use it to do my homework.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.800
- You can use it to make also things like

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.418
a security detector, an alarm.

00:01:49.418 --> 00:01:52.180
(devices beeping)

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.380
- One of the really exciting
things that we've seen is

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.270
when you give students
access to these tools,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.510
and then they understand
the concepts really well,

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.670
these students begin to innovate.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.330
One of such examples is a
student called Princess.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.290
She didn't have access to electricity.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:07.790
After using the Science Set and learning

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.550
about electrical circuits, she
came up with her light box.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.380
And now four of her friends
can come to her house,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.843
and they can collaborate
on their homework.

00:02:15.843 --> 00:02:16.676
(inspiring music)

00:02:16.676 --> 00:02:19.230
- [Narrator] While the dream
is big for this mad scientist,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.250
up next is something
actually pretty small.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.090
- [Charles] We are creating a microscope

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.240
that costs us a little
less than five dollars.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.960
The idea for the microscope

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.710
is to be able to use
really affordable materials

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:34.470
to be able to create something

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.210
that can magnify up to about 10x to 45x,

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.060
so the students can view onion
cells and things like that.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.990
It's humbling, I would say;

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.190
it feels very humbling to see
students use the Science Set.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.050
We believe that in the
not too distant future

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.340
we're gonna have really,
really awesome scientists

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.333
coming from Africa.

00:02:53.246 --> 00:02:55.572
(children chattering and clapping)

00:02:55.572 --> 00:02:58.489
(soft piano music)

00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:02.276
(electronic tones)

